<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Valois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1328">1328</a>–<a href="/years/1589">1589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Valois was a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a> that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1328, when Philip VI succeeded the last direct Capetian, until 1589. It led France through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, and its junior lines included the Valois dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. On the death of Henry III in 1589 the throne passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-valois.jpg" alt="House of Valois" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlodangio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Philippe_de_Valois.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Valois descended from Charles, Count of Valois (1270-1325), the second surviving son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a>. Philip IV left three sons, Louis, Philip and Charles, who each became king in turn and each died young without a surviving male heir. When Charles IV died in 1328 three candidates had a plausible claim: Philip, Count of Valois, the closest heir in the male line; Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, daughter of Louis X; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, son of Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The French magnates rejected a claim passing through a woman and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip of Valois</a> as Philip VI. Joan received the Kingdom of Navarre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Philip VI confiscated the Duchy of Aquitaine in 1337, and Edward III claimed the French throne himself, which helped launch the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. The French were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> in 1346 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356, where John II was captured.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> they recovered most of their territory, leaving the English only Calais, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> by 1380. Charles VI's madness let rivalry between the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> grow into the Armagnac-Burgundian Civil War, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> in 1415 the Treaty of Troyes made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> heir to the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> in 1429 and had Charles VII crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. By 1453, after the English defeat at Castillon, only Calais remained to them.</p><p>Charles VII created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first standing army since Roman times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> subdued the great nobles, who had formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal">League of the Public Weal</a> against him in 1465, by intrigue and diplomacy, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> died in 1477 he seized the duchy of Burgundy. Charles VIII invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494 to press an Angevin claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, and died in 1498 without heirs, ending the senior line. His cousin Louis XII, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a>, who was captured at Pavia in 1525, and Henry II continued the wars, which ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> in 1559.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Henry II died in a jousting accident in 1559, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, Charles IX and Henry III ruled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-guise">House of Guise</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. The St Bartholomew's Day massacre took place in 1572. Henry III had the Duke of Guise assassinated, and was himself killed by an assassin in 1589. With his death the male line of the Valois was extinct after 261 years on the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry of Navarre</a>, as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a>, the senior surviving Capetian branch, became Henry IV. Junior Valois lines held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, Anjou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Alençon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Valois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of Flanders, then the Avesnes, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the Wittelsbachs from 1345 and the <b>Valois</b> dukes of Burgundy from 1433, before the Habsburgs inherited it in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Military power grew dramatically under the <b>Valois</b> dukes, who could draw on the wealth of Flanders and the Low Countries as well as the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 13th and 14th centuries the county passed by marriage to the Flemish counts, including Louis of Nevers, and then to a younger branch of the <b>Valois</b> dukes of Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nevers">County of Nevers</a></li></ul></section>
